# XSH sem_clockwait() and sem_timedwait()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The pages of sem_clockwait and sem_timedwait are one file, so both
# functions live in this module. Every test that is not about a single
# function runs three forms: sem_timedwait(), sem_clockwait() with
# CLOCK_REALTIME and with CLOCK_MONOTONIC. A timeout is checked against
# its clock from below only (it must not end before the time); the upper
# bounds only detect a hang. The validity of abstime when the semaphore
# can be locked at once need not be checked, so it is not tested.

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.001
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       Each of the three forms, called on a semaphore of value 2 with
           an absolute time long passed (the epoch), returns 0 and lowers
           the value by one (a timeout never beats a lock that can be had
           at once).
test       t/001-immediate.c

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.002
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#ERRORS/ETIMEDOUT
class      required
says       Each form on a semaphore of value 0, with an absolute time 200
           ms ahead on its clock (the real-time clock for sem_timedwait()),
           returns -1 with ETIMEDOUT, and that clock then reads at least
           the absolute time.
test       t/002-times-out.c

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.003
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#ERRORS/ETIMEDOUT
class      required
says       Each form on a semaphore of value 0, with an absolute time that
           has already passed (the epoch; one second ago), returns -1 with
           ETIMEDOUT.
test       t/003-expired.c

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.004
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       After a timed wait has failed with ETIMEDOUT, the semaphore is
           unchanged: it is still 0, and one post then gives exactly one
           successful lock.
test       t/004-state-unchanged.c

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.005
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       Each form, blocked in a thread on a semaphore of value 0 with a
           timeout 30 s ahead, does not return before a post and returns 0
           after the sem_post(); the post is consumed.
timeout    90
test       t/005-post-wakes.c

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.006
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
says       Each form on a semaphore of value 0, with an absolute time a few
           seconds ahead but a nanoseconds field of -1, 1000000000 or
           2000000000, returns -1 with EINVAL.
note       A system that does not check the field waits the seconds and
           reports ETIMEDOUT, which fails.
test       t/006-bad-nanoseconds.c

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.007
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
says       sem_clockwait() on a semaphore of value 0 with a clock_id that
           names no clock (one clock_gettime() rejects with EINVAL) returns
           -1 with EINVAL.
note       UNRESOLVED if every candidate identifier names a clock.
test       t/007-bad-clock.c

assertion  XSH.sem_clockwait.008
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#ERRORS/EINTR
anchor     functions/sem_clockwait.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       When a handler runs in a thread in a timed wait on a semaphore of
           value 0 (timeout 1.5 s ahead, no post), the call returns -1 with
           EINTR, or goes on and returns -1 with ETIMEDOUT after the time;
           it does not return 0, and the semaphore is unchanged.
note       The handler is installed without SA_RESTART and must be seen to
           run (UNRESOLVED if not).
timeout    90
test       t/008-signal.c
